5. Direct centsFromDecimalString brute force (20M random int64 cents, standalone Go program, not the package's algorithm assumed correct): round-trip float64(cents)/100 -> %.2f -> reparse holds for all magnitudes up to ~$1 trillion/month; only fails above ~$100 trillion/month (float64 mantissa exhaustion near 2^53), many orders of magnitude outside any realistic household budget input and outside the oracle's own domain ($1-$200,000/month). Documented as a theoretical residual, not treated as a failing case given the app's domain (personal retirement/budget planner).
6. Consumer sweep: grepped all consumers of models.ExpenseBreakdownItem/SubItems (`grep -rln "ExpenseBreakdown"`) — only web/templates/components/whatif/budget-analysis.html renders SubItems, via the same formatMoney used for the total; engine.CalculateExpenseBreakdown / monte_carlo.go use an unrelated Essential/Discretionary/Total struct with no phase sub-rows; charts.js's renderMajorExpenseBreakdown is a same-named-but-unrelated JS chart function. No second consumer to diverge.
7. centsFromDecimalString parsing robustness: v=0 and v=-0.0 both yield 0 (int64 has no negative zero, confirmed); no scientific notation possible from %.2f (only %e/%g produce it in Go); whole-part string never carries a sign since math.Abs is applied before formatting.

